WEBVTT

00:01.120 --> 00:03.240
[theme song playing]

01:16.600 --> 01:20.120
He was murdered this morning
in a Street Show in the Patch.

01:20.200 --> 01:24.000
He was Irish. Is he a person of interest
to your department, sir?

01:24.080 --> 01:25.440
Do you read the papers?

01:25.520 --> 01:27.560
Birmingham papers.

01:27.640 --> 01:29.800
I recommend the Daily Mail.

01:29.880 --> 01:32.000
It'll broaden your mind.

01:33.360 --> 01:37.360
Now, if you read the national press,
you would know

01:37.600 --> 01:41.280
that the King has offered
the rebel Irish a treaty.

01:41.360 --> 01:43.680
Some of the Fenians want to accept it.

01:43.920 --> 01:45.880
The IRA do not.

01:45.960 --> 01:49.440
The IRA have a long
and glorious relationship

01:50.280 --> 01:52.480
with the garrote.

01:52.560 --> 01:54.200
Oh dear.

01:54.280 --> 01:56.840
Are they fighting amongst themselves, sir?

01:59.960 --> 02:01.800
- That'll be all.
- Right, sir.

02:01.880 --> 02:04.960
I'm going to be up and down
from London quite a bit.

02:05.040 --> 02:07.360
I'm gonna need your help with lodgings.

02:07.440 --> 02:10.640
You don't want to stay
at the previous lodgings, sir?

02:11.320 --> 02:13.240
Too many bad memories I suppose.

02:13.320 --> 02:15.120
You should understand, Moss,

02:15.200 --> 02:19.120
I have no regrets about my
previous mission in this city.

02:19.200 --> 02:20.440
No, sir?

02:21.920 --> 02:25.240
I imagine you boys have
quite a laugh at my expense.

02:25.640 --> 02:26.960
A laugh, sir?

02:27.040 --> 02:29.960
You think it's funny
that I developed feelings.

02:30.040 --> 02:32.480
No idea what you're
talking about, sir.

02:32.560 --> 02:34.240
I have no regrets

02:34.320 --> 02:36.960
about what happened
with Miss Burgess.

02:37.040 --> 02:39.480
No bad memories.

02:41.080 --> 02:42.120
Right, sir.

02:43.240 --> 02:44.560
[typewriter clicking]

02:45.680 --> 02:47.840
He's losing his bloody mind.

02:51.480 --> 02:52.680
[sighs]

02:57.000 --> 02:58.200
[clamoring]

03:06.600 --> 03:07.760
Next.

03:10.840 --> 03:12.240
- Go.
- Next.

03:29.920 --> 03:32.400
[Arthur] All right, then.
[Johnny] Name?

03:33.000 --> 03:34.640
The Digbeth Kid.

03:34.720 --> 03:36.040
[sniffs]

03:36.320 --> 03:37.640
The Dig...

03:38.080 --> 03:39.680
What are you? A boxer?

03:40.920 --> 03:42.920
Digbeth Kid, like Billy the Kid.

03:48.000 --> 03:50.320
Spend a lot of time
at the pictures, eh?

03:50.760 --> 03:53.640
Cowboy pictures? Tom Mix? Yeah?

03:58.120 --> 04:00.000
- What are you...
- Wait! Wait!

04:00.400 --> 04:01.960
It's not a real gun.

04:02.640 --> 04:05.320
- Let's have a look then.
- It's made of wood.

04:07.520 --> 04:08.680
[chuckles]

04:10.200 --> 04:14.800
Bang. Bang. Very nice.

04:15.160 --> 04:16.960
Where'd you get the gun belt?

04:18.240 --> 04:21.560
My sister made it out of
an old blacksmith's apron.

04:21.640 --> 04:23.600
My mom did most
of the stitching.

04:23.680 --> 04:27.400
- She's not my real mom but...
- But she does what mothers do.

04:27.480 --> 04:29.080
So she made you a gun belt.

04:29.240 --> 04:31.040
All right there, mother's boy.

04:31.120 --> 04:33.760
There's the door, on you go.
We're looking...

04:34.880 --> 04:36.560
Have you ever been arrested?

04:36.640 --> 04:37.840
- Yeah.
- Yeah?

04:39.960 --> 04:41.360
- No.
- Good.

04:42.520 --> 04:46.120
You're the first bloke in here
today with no criminal record.

04:46.200 --> 04:48.480
Arthur, we can stand him up.

04:48.560 --> 04:50.160
You know what that means?

04:51.160 --> 04:54.600
The new Home Secretary
wants something done

04:54.680 --> 04:56.040
about illegal gambling.

04:56.120 --> 04:58.200
- Damn right.
- About bloody time.

04:58.280 --> 05:01.080
We help our coppers make
their convictions

05:01.160 --> 05:03.840
by having men stood up to be arrested.

05:03.920 --> 05:06.840
First offense, you'll get a week inside.

05:07.200 --> 05:09.800
We'll give you five quid for your trouble.

05:10.040 --> 05:11.720
How does that sound, cowboy?

05:12.120 --> 05:13.400
Yeah.

05:15.280 --> 05:16.600
Good...

05:16.680 --> 05:19.560
Write your address down
for Finn on your way out.

05:22.000 --> 05:23.280
I can't write.

05:23.360 --> 05:25.960
That's all right, Finn can't read.

05:26.040 --> 05:28.160
Don't worry.
Tell him your address.

05:28.240 --> 05:29.880
We'll send someone round.

05:29.960 --> 05:31.280
If...

05:31.360 --> 05:34.160
the Sheriff don't run you
out of town first.

05:35.960 --> 05:38.360
[laughs]

05:40.720 --> 05:41.880
Good man!

05:43.480 --> 05:46.520
Oh, I don't know, Tom.
Kids these days...

05:49.240 --> 05:52.920
They didn't fight.
So they're different.

05:53.320 --> 05:54.800
They stay kids.

05:58.840 --> 06:00.160
How was it?

06:00.720 --> 06:03.040
I was starving,
I'd have eaten anything.

06:04.120 --> 06:05.600
But, really it was super.

06:07.920 --> 06:09.080
Super.

06:11.520 --> 06:13.800
So, are you a cook?

06:15.120 --> 06:17.080
Not a cook, no.

06:24.280 --> 06:26.080
The man who came
to the house,

06:26.160 --> 06:28.800
He was driving a posh car,
he looks rich.

06:29.280 --> 06:30.560
What does he do?

06:31.960 --> 06:34.120
- He works with horses.
- Really?

06:35.600 --> 06:36.960
I love horses.

06:37.640 --> 06:39.240
- I've got a bay mare.
- No?

06:39.320 --> 06:40.840
I ride it all the time.

06:43.000 --> 06:44.360
Then it's in the blood.

06:49.400 --> 06:51.440
I've got about a million questions.

06:51.520 --> 06:52.920
So do I.

06:53.320 --> 06:55.600
I've had different pictures
in my head.

06:55.800 --> 06:57.680
Well, here I am.

07:20.000 --> 07:22.240
It's all right that it's me, isn't it?

07:23.320 --> 07:24.800
I don't have any choice.

07:26.960 --> 07:29.360
I mean, you are who you are, aren't you?

07:31.000 --> 07:32.400
We don't choose.

07:32.960 --> 07:34.200
And that's it.

07:35.960 --> 07:37.280
Yeah. That's right.

07:40.240 --> 07:41.640
God gave you to me.

07:44.560 --> 07:46.160
People took you away.

07:49.480 --> 07:52.320
And it is all right
that it's me? Isn't it?

07:54.200 --> 07:55.720
In a place like this.

07:57.680 --> 07:59.120
I thought it'd be worse.

07:59.200 --> 08:00.200
[laughs]

08:01.520 --> 08:04.360
[Arthur] Run for the hills!
It's the Digbeth Kid!

08:04.440 --> 08:07.800
Get out of town kid or I will shoot your
fucking head off!

08:07.880 --> 08:09.800
Time's up!
[imitates gun firing]

08:09.880 --> 08:11.680
You're dead. Go down. John!

08:24.960 --> 08:26.960
All right then, Polly.

08:27.160 --> 08:29.320
Who's this?
[Snickering]

08:29.400 --> 08:31.240
Gentlemen?

08:31.320 --> 08:33.440
This is your cousin.

08:33.760 --> 08:36.040
Polly's son, Michael.

08:43.960 --> 08:46.120
- Pleased to meet you.
- Sure.

08:46.200 --> 08:50.000
I'm Arthur. You've met me.
I used to throw you out of the window

08:50.080 --> 08:51.560
so John could catch you.

08:51.640 --> 08:53.680
I used to put you in a shoe box

08:53.760 --> 08:55.960
and kick you down Watery Lane.

08:56.040 --> 08:57.760
I bet you're glad to be back.

08:57.840 --> 09:00.200
[laughs] I don't remember any of it.

09:00.280 --> 09:02.920
All I remember is the day
they took me away.

09:15.200 --> 09:17.000
Well you're here now, son.

09:18.600 --> 09:20.320
Welcome to the Shelby family.

09:20.400 --> 09:22.280
Later we'll show you the ropes.

09:22.840 --> 09:24.840
Yeah, we'll show you what's what.

09:26.080 --> 09:27.840
Let's leave him be for now?

09:29.400 --> 09:30.840
Come on boys.

09:31.720 --> 09:33.040
Nice suit.

09:33.120 --> 09:34.360
[chuckles]

09:38.840 --> 09:40.080
They seem nice.

09:42.840 --> 09:45.080
[machine pounding]

09:49.360 --> 09:52.200
[John] Here he is,
Mr. Inconspicuous.

09:52.280 --> 09:53.840
Corporal Billy Kitchen.

09:53.920 --> 09:55.680
Bloody grand to see you.

09:55.760 --> 09:56.960
How are you feeling?

09:57.040 --> 09:58.920
I've just come out of bandages.

09:59.000 --> 10:00.720
Ready for active service.

10:00.800 --> 10:03.440
- Thanks for seeing me here.
- It's all right.

10:03.520 --> 10:07.840
You don't have to stand in line for us.
We're looking for men like you.

10:07.920 --> 10:10.160
But you have to pass
the medical first.

10:10.280 --> 10:12.040
Sheffield mob showed up

10:12.120 --> 10:14.320
at Wincanton trotting track.

10:14.440 --> 10:15.800
I took a bullet.

10:19.000 --> 10:21.000
When do the bandages come off?

10:21.080 --> 10:23.200
When I take 'em off.
[scoffs]

10:23.520 --> 10:25.600
One week. Not before.

10:25.680 --> 10:27.200
You'll smell no rot.

10:27.280 --> 10:29.280
There's no gangrene.

10:30.000 --> 10:32.240
It's been a long time, eh, Billy?

10:33.560 --> 10:37.080
- What did you do with your medals?
- Threw them in the cut.

10:37.160 --> 10:38.720
Same as you.

10:39.920 --> 10:44.240
It was never a hardship to have you
Black Country boys on our left flank.

10:44.320 --> 10:46.880
And you Brummies did
all right on our right.

10:46.960 --> 10:48.320
Damn right.

10:49.680 --> 10:52.800
I want you to be head
of a brigade, Billy.

10:52.880 --> 10:55.560
It'll be Brigadier Kitchen
from now on.

10:55.640 --> 10:58.600
You'll have a hundred men
under your command.

10:59.680 --> 11:02.920
We've got a member
of the Kitchen family on the payroll,

11:03.000 --> 11:06.240
Hope your brothers will let
our boats pass Black Country

11:06.320 --> 11:08.480
without being held up and robbed.

11:08.560 --> 11:11.400
I don't know what
you're talking about.

11:11.480 --> 11:13.960
Round up any good men you can trust.

11:14.040 --> 11:15.640
And put the word out.

11:15.720 --> 11:18.800
Black Country and Brummie boys
are on the same side.

11:18.880 --> 11:20.600
That'll be the bloody day.

11:20.680 --> 11:22.640
Well, this is the bloody day.

11:24.680 --> 11:26.440
London, man.

11:26.520 --> 11:27.960
There for the taking.

11:29.480 --> 11:32.200
[indistinct chattering]

11:41.520 --> 11:43.560
Tommy, I need to use the telephone.

11:43.640 --> 11:45.640
Polly said you had one
in the pub.

11:47.240 --> 11:49.760
All right. Be quick.
I have business.

11:52.760 --> 11:55.560
- Sir.
- Polly said you own this place.

11:55.640 --> 11:58.400
She said you own lots
of different businesses.

11:58.480 --> 12:00.520
You call her Polly or mum?

12:00.600 --> 12:03.040
I can't get used to
calling her mum yet.

12:04.280 --> 12:05.480
[sighs]

12:06.600 --> 12:08.880
- Who are you calling?
- My mother.

12:08.960 --> 12:11.640
- I mean...
- I know what you mean.

12:12.920 --> 12:14.760
What are you going to tell her?

12:14.840 --> 12:16.800
I'm going to tell her
where I am.

12:16.920 --> 12:19.920
And now you're going to tell her
you're going home.

12:22.040 --> 12:24.280
Polly said I could stay
for a few days.

12:24.360 --> 12:26.800
- You want to stay?
- I only just arrived.

12:31.120 --> 12:33.800
- What age are you, Michael?
- Seventeen.

12:33.880 --> 12:37.040
Seventeen. Which means
it's not up to you, right?

12:37.120 --> 12:40.080
I'm eighteen in a few weeks.
I make up my own mind.

12:40.160 --> 12:41.480
You smoke?

12:41.960 --> 12:43.080
No.

12:45.600 --> 12:46.760
Drink?

12:51.360 --> 12:53.320
You're going to call your mother.

12:53.400 --> 12:55.960
Tell her you're getting the
next train home.

12:56.080 --> 12:59.000
When you get there,
write a letter to Polly saying

12:59.080 --> 13:01.360
when you're 18, you'll come back here.

13:01.440 --> 13:02.560
I just told you.

13:04.400 --> 13:06.080
I make up my own mind.

13:08.720 --> 13:10.120
Do you know what we do?

13:11.400 --> 13:12.480
Michael?

13:14.240 --> 13:17.200
We, Shelbys?

13:18.680 --> 13:21.280
Yes. I think I know what you do.

13:22.640 --> 13:23.880
Yeah.

13:24.640 --> 13:26.280
You've got smart eyes.

13:28.040 --> 13:29.760
But you're young,

13:29.840 --> 13:32.000
so you think what
we do is all right.

13:33.600 --> 13:35.280
It's not all right.

13:35.640 --> 13:36.840
People get hurt.

13:40.160 --> 13:41.920
Now call your mother.

13:42.280 --> 13:44.920
- And I will drive you to the station.
- No.

13:51.320 --> 13:52.600
In my village,

13:53.520 --> 13:55.560
there's this little wishing well.

13:56.640 --> 13:58.240
It's made of white bricks.

13:58.320 --> 14:00.760
Right in the middle
of the village green.

14:01.720 --> 14:03.720
Everybody says how pretty it is.

14:05.680 --> 14:07.280
But I swear to God

14:08.280 --> 14:10.720
if I spend another day
in that village

14:11.160 --> 14:13.800
I'm going to blow it up
with dynamites.

14:14.160 --> 14:18.320
Probably blow my hands off with it
but it will be worth it.

14:18.800 --> 14:22.320
Just to see all those
pretty white bricks

14:22.720 --> 14:25.320
spread over the pretty
village green.

14:32.800 --> 14:34.040
[Scoffs]

14:36.800 --> 14:38.120
[exhales]

14:38.640 --> 14:41.880
Yep. You're Polly's son all right.

14:50.120 --> 14:52.360
[people chattering]

14:52.480 --> 14:54.680
[fast paced music playing]

14:55.720 --> 14:58.200
All right boys,
let's go to work.

14:59.280 --> 15:01.280
Let's be having it!

15:02.440 --> 15:04.280
Don't wait!

15:07.120 --> 15:09.080
Keep it moving.

15:12.240 --> 15:13.720
Move!

15:27.600 --> 15:29.720
[indistinct chatter]

15:30.000 --> 15:31.560
Next lad!

15:34.520 --> 15:36.880
- Name?
- Abbey Heath.

15:36.960 --> 15:39.480
- Abbey Heath. Profession?
- Baker.

15:40.560 --> 15:43.480
Good lad, fill it out, fuck off.

15:43.800 --> 15:45.440
Next lad!

15:48.440 --> 15:50.800
- Name?
- Buddy Halls.

15:50.880 --> 15:55.360
- Buddy Halls. Right, profession?
- Baker.

15:55.440 --> 15:57.880
Good lad, fill it out, fuck off.

15:59.240 --> 16:00.720
Next lad!

16:02.520 --> 16:04.280
- Name?
- Billy Kitchen.

16:04.360 --> 16:08.320
- Billy Kitchen. Occupation?
- Head baker.

16:14.720 --> 16:16.120
Fill it out.

16:23.760 --> 16:25.440
Tommy Shelby, mate.

16:25.640 --> 16:29.440
Never give power to the big man,
what did I tell you? Mmm?

16:29.520 --> 16:32.960
Never give power to the big man.

16:33.240 --> 16:34.600
Next lad!

16:36.600 --> 16:37.800
Name?

16:38.880 --> 16:42.760
All right boys, you've now all been
enrolled as bakers

16:43.200 --> 16:45.720
in the Aerated Bread
Company of Camden Town.

16:45.800 --> 16:49.760
If anyone asks, that's what
you do. You're bakers.

16:49.840 --> 16:52.840
The coppers in Camden Town
are on our side

16:52.920 --> 16:57.080
but north or south, you show them
that piece of paper.

16:57.160 --> 17:01.600
Tell them you've come down
from north to find work. To break strikes.

17:01.680 --> 17:04.920
Tell them you're fascists
if you have to.

17:05.200 --> 17:06.960
We're finding lodging for you

17:07.040 --> 17:09.880
but for now, you'll sleep
here in the bakery.

17:10.600 --> 17:12.320
Don't touch any of the bread,

17:12.440 --> 17:14.440
it'll most likely explode.

17:16.520 --> 17:17.880
Any questions?

17:19.200 --> 17:20.600
Yes?

17:21.080 --> 17:22.880
I haven't even seen any bread.

17:23.000 --> 17:24.640
[all laughing]

17:30.680 --> 17:31.840
[clears throat]

17:48.800 --> 17:49.800
[screams]

17:59.040 --> 18:01.000
He'll wake up.

18:01.080 --> 18:03.000
He won't have any teeth left.

18:03.080 --> 18:05.560
But he will be a wiser man for it.

18:08.280 --> 18:12.520
And the last thing he will remember is
your funny little joke. Won't he?

18:20.120 --> 18:21.480
Right!

18:23.240 --> 18:25.760
There are fucking rules here, yeah?

18:25.840 --> 18:28.680
There are fucking rules
for a fucking reason.

18:28.760 --> 18:31.560
Quite simply they have
to be obeyed. All right?

18:33.880 --> 18:35.520
Rule number one.

18:36.200 --> 18:39.800
The distinction between
bread and rum...

18:39.880 --> 18:42.080
That is not discussed.

18:44.640 --> 18:46.360
Rule number two.

18:49.720 --> 18:55.040
Anything right, that your superior
officer says to you

18:55.120 --> 19:00.240
or any of your other fucking superior
officers say to you, yeah?

19:00.400 --> 19:02.520
Not discussed!

19:05.840 --> 19:09.080
Rule number three,
four, five, six,

19:09.160 --> 19:11.160
seven, eight, nine.
I don't care...

19:11.240 --> 19:15.400
For the rest of your fucking
miserable, measly lives yeah?

19:15.480 --> 19:18.080
Because I like you,

19:18.160 --> 19:22.160
I am also a complete
fucking sodomite.

19:27.440 --> 19:28.800
Jewish women.

19:31.200 --> 19:33.040
You do not go anywhere
near them

19:33.120 --> 19:36.840
because Jewish women for you
are off the fucking menu.

19:39.120 --> 19:40.560
I think that's fair.

19:47.960 --> 19:49.360
Hmm...

20:00.960 --> 20:03.400
Hm... Oh, that's it then.

20:04.880 --> 20:07.080
Forgive me.
I interrupted you.

20:14.280 --> 20:15.600
Pick him up.

20:22.440 --> 20:24.240
Get them out of here

20:24.320 --> 20:26.200
and make this fucking work.

20:30.160 --> 20:32.280
Don't fucking wait.

20:32.360 --> 20:35.080
You're meant to be
fucking soldiers.

20:35.200 --> 20:38.000
You're a fucking disgrace.

20:38.320 --> 20:39.880
Go!

21:24.720 --> 21:26.120
[footsteps approaching]

21:27.760 --> 21:28.760
[door opens]

21:32.160 --> 21:33.160
[laughs]

21:35.200 --> 21:37.720
You've got a key?

21:38.040 --> 21:40.320
- I kept a spare.
- Give it to me.

21:47.360 --> 21:49.600
Could do with some paint, eh?

21:49.680 --> 21:52.040
Yeah. When I decide.

21:52.120 --> 21:53.120
[clears throat]

21:57.240 --> 21:58.240
[sighs]

21:59.520 --> 22:01.080
What is it that you want?

22:01.160 --> 22:04.480
Just came by to say hello.

22:04.920 --> 22:08.000
Tommy Shelby never goes
anywhere for no reason.

22:09.120 --> 22:10.320
[SIGHS] Fine.

22:12.720 --> 22:16.600
I've got eight hundred pounds left in the
Shelby property fund.

22:16.880 --> 22:20.280
I need somebody down here
to look for suitable properties.

22:20.360 --> 22:21.600
To rent?

22:22.920 --> 22:24.000
Yes.

22:24.800 --> 22:26.440
To poor people?

22:26.680 --> 22:29.880
Ten to a room.
No repairs, no water.

22:30.160 --> 22:33.320
If they complain, you just send
Arthur and the boys.

22:35.080 --> 22:37.800
You know, I give advice
down at the library.

22:38.240 --> 22:40.640
Families thrown on the street.

22:41.720 --> 22:43.920
It's men like you we're fighting.

22:47.200 --> 22:49.880
Well, anyway, I was just passing.

22:49.960 --> 22:51.400
Thanks for the tea.

22:55.280 --> 22:56.440
Tommy?

23:00.960 --> 23:04.320
There are always men
outside watching the house.

23:04.720 --> 23:06.760
Yeah. Gangsters of the worst kind.

23:06.840 --> 23:08.680
They're there to keep you safe.

23:08.760 --> 23:10.800
No, others. They look like coppers.

23:12.960 --> 23:15.040
Well, they're on your side as well.

23:15.720 --> 23:19.760
- I don't have a fucking side.
- Ada. Yes, you do.

23:20.960 --> 23:24.560
And, anyway, if I thought there
was no one watching the house,

23:24.640 --> 23:25.920
I couldn't sleep.

23:28.000 --> 23:29.120
Cheerio then.

23:32.400 --> 23:33.400
[door closes]

23:37.880 --> 23:39.600
[car tires screeching]

23:44.880 --> 23:47.200
That was great, well done.

23:47.280 --> 23:48.960
Out we get then.

23:59.120 --> 24:01.800
This house is mine.

24:04.200 --> 24:05.960
I've taken on a maid.

24:06.360 --> 24:08.440
She's made up
the big room for you.

24:09.080 --> 24:11.600
Come on, let's have a look.

24:15.840 --> 24:17.200
[door closes]

24:19.720 --> 24:21.640
We could spend the week here.

24:26.240 --> 24:29.040
You know, get to know
each other again.

24:34.560 --> 24:36.840
I've got ham. Do you like ham?

24:37.720 --> 24:39.240
I like ham, yes.

24:42.240 --> 24:44.240
- I've got a maid.
- Yes, you said.

24:46.840 --> 24:48.280
She's upstairs.

24:49.960 --> 24:51.600
She could give us some tea.

24:51.680 --> 24:54.520
Look, I ring this bell. Watch.

24:54.680 --> 24:55.720
[Bell dinging]

24:56.000 --> 24:57.480
[foot steps approaching]

25:00.480 --> 25:01.720
Yes, madam?

25:06.600 --> 25:08.280
I think we'd like some tea.

25:12.000 --> 25:13.680
[foot steps retreating]

25:15.280 --> 25:16.680
You get used to it.

25:17.680 --> 25:20.560
[upbeat music playing]

25:23.000 --> 25:24.520
[Sniffing]

26:00.360 --> 26:03.600
The cleaning job's gone.
The vacancy's filled.

26:03.680 --> 26:05.200
I'm not a cleaner.

26:06.880 --> 26:09.800
But I have come to clean
away some dirt.

26:14.360 --> 26:17.160
Why don't you put that away
before it goes off?

26:17.240 --> 26:20.440
You killed my son.

26:20.520 --> 26:23.320
You Peaky bastard!

26:25.040 --> 26:29.880
You beat him and beat him
and beat him.

26:32.040 --> 26:34.960
- Your son was a boxer.
- No!

26:37.880 --> 26:39.800
He was a boy...

26:41.440 --> 26:45.280
...who got into a ring
with an animal.

26:47.440 --> 26:50.560
- Yeah, ain't that the truth?
- I've come to stop you.

26:52.160 --> 26:56.240
Because the coppers
and nobody else will.

26:56.320 --> 26:58.480
[panting]

27:05.440 --> 27:07.360
But if you're going to use it...

27:08.040 --> 27:09.920
...point that thing at my head.

27:11.080 --> 27:12.960
That's where the trouble is.

27:15.240 --> 27:18.000
Gut shot soldiers take
half a day to die.

27:19.960 --> 27:21.720
I've seen them.

27:21.800 --> 27:25.600
Walking around with their guts in their
arms like dirty washing.

27:27.840 --> 27:29.800
Hold that gun up and do it.

27:30.080 --> 27:31.600
Fucking do it!

27:32.520 --> 27:33.760
Do it!

27:33.840 --> 27:35.760
[breathing heavily]

27:47.840 --> 27:49.600
I'm going to finish my drink.

27:55.160 --> 27:56.720
You can finish it with me.

27:56.800 --> 27:57.800
[gunshot]

27:59.880 --> 28:00.880
[gasps]

28:01.000 --> 28:02.240
Come on!

28:04.440 --> 28:06.560
[gasping]

28:07.400 --> 28:09.680
[crying]

28:20.680 --> 28:22.360
[Crying]

28:30.800 --> 28:32.440
Now, look.

28:32.520 --> 28:33.680
Look at me.

28:35.880 --> 28:37.280
We got a fund.

28:39.120 --> 28:40.760
You'll get paid weekly.

28:45.320 --> 28:47.440
I know it won't bring
back your son.

28:50.640 --> 28:51.880
I know that.

28:54.920 --> 28:56.600
And you have other sons.

28:59.320 --> 29:00.800
He died.

29:01.800 --> 29:03.960
We'll find them work.

29:04.040 --> 29:06.600
And you won't have
to work because...

29:06.680 --> 29:07.800
[screaming]

29:16.400 --> 29:21.040
Somebody has got
to stop you people!

29:43.280 --> 29:45.240
You broke the fucking mirror.

29:45.520 --> 29:47.640
Seven years bad luck that is.

29:54.800 --> 29:57.720
[Tommy] There'll be another 4
boat loads tomorrow.

29:57.800 --> 30:00.600
What's up, Charlie?
Business is good?

30:00.680 --> 30:03.880
This isn't business,
this is bloody work.

30:03.960 --> 30:06.000
Cigarettes and booze
is all right.

30:06.080 --> 30:08.800
But this manufactured stuff,
it's heavy.

30:08.880 --> 30:10.760
I'm not even sure it's stolen.

30:12.280 --> 30:14.600
Some of it is legally purchased.

30:14.680 --> 30:17.120
One day, all of it will be.

30:17.200 --> 30:19.080
It's like having a fucking job.

30:19.160 --> 30:21.000
Easy with them fan belts.

30:22.440 --> 30:24.360
Wipe that smile off your face.

30:24.440 --> 30:26.360
I want another pound a boat.

30:29.080 --> 30:30.360
Done.

30:32.560 --> 30:36.080
You don't even fight me anymore!

30:36.160 --> 30:39.960
It's no sport getting through the
Black Country with this truce.

30:40.040 --> 30:42.960
They just fucking wave
at you from the bank.

30:43.040 --> 30:45.240
You just wave back, all right?

30:46.400 --> 30:48.960
And all these fucking cars.

30:49.040 --> 30:51.280
When did you last ride a horse, Tom?

31:03.840 --> 31:04.840
[knock on door]

31:05.280 --> 31:06.320
Aye?

31:06.400 --> 31:09.880
These are just dry, Mr. Campbell.

31:09.960 --> 31:11.760
Major Campbell.

31:11.880 --> 31:13.200
Beg pardon?

31:13.920 --> 31:15.320
It doesn't matter.

31:20.920 --> 31:23.880
Are there any other Irishmen
lodging in this house?

31:23.960 --> 31:25.640
No, Mr. Campbell,

31:25.720 --> 31:28.040
I keep a respectable house.

31:28.120 --> 31:30.160
With your special exception.

31:30.240 --> 31:32.080
I have no Irish here.

31:34.880 --> 31:38.320
Well, let me know if any
come inquiring after rooms.

31:39.800 --> 31:43.080
Even if you turn them away,
let me know would you?

31:43.160 --> 31:45.600
You're not expecting trouble,
I hope.

31:45.680 --> 31:48.760
They only said you was
a policeman of some sort.

31:48.840 --> 31:52.120
No, I'm not expecting
any trouble.

31:53.800 --> 31:57.600
Is there a cafe nearby
that serves chops...

31:58.200 --> 31:59.440
lamb or pork?

31:59.720 --> 32:02.200
There is a chophouse
two streets away.

32:02.480 --> 32:04.960
But I myself serve sandwiches.

32:05.240 --> 32:08.040
Egg and cress or ham.

32:08.400 --> 32:10.400
Or anything that pleases you.

32:12.160 --> 32:14.280
No. No, no.

32:14.440 --> 32:18.040
I think I'll go out
and get some air.

32:29.160 --> 32:32.280
Our scheduled meeting
is not until Sunday.

32:32.360 --> 32:34.920
Sorry to bring it forward.

32:35.000 --> 32:38.200
You just wanted to show me that you know
where I live.

32:38.280 --> 32:40.040
Am I supposed to be impressed?

32:41.440 --> 32:45.240
You know, Mr. Churchill
was impressed.

32:45.600 --> 32:47.080
Yes, he was.

32:48.400 --> 32:50.480
All my demands are being met.

32:52.280 --> 32:53.960
Did he tell you?

32:54.040 --> 32:55.160
Yes.

32:55.680 --> 32:59.560
Yes. We had a meeting
and your letter was read aloud.

33:00.800 --> 33:03.760
There was quite a lot
of hilarity at the meeting.

33:03.840 --> 33:06.040
The gentlemen all find
it quite funny.

33:06.120 --> 33:10.280
[scoffs] I see. The 'gentlemen'
found it funny.

33:10.360 --> 33:12.840
Tell me this, Mr. Campbell.
[Clears throat]

33:13.040 --> 33:16.080
Do the gentlemen
sometimes find you funny?

33:21.960 --> 33:25.400
We will discuss our business
on Sunday.

33:25.480 --> 33:28.600
On the agreed day.
At the allotted time.

33:28.680 --> 33:31.560
And at the place that has
already been determined.

33:32.800 --> 33:34.600
You've made your point.

33:34.680 --> 33:36.320
Good night, Mr. Shelby.

33:37.600 --> 33:41.080
There's another reason
I came to see you tonight.

33:41.640 --> 33:44.680
I just didn't want you
to be made a fool of is all.

33:45.560 --> 33:50.680
The lodgings chosen for you
by the police department.

33:51.000 --> 33:52.560
How shall I put this?

33:53.760 --> 33:55.840
Well the landlady used to run

33:55.920 --> 33:58.440
the most famous whore house in Stetchford.

33:59.640 --> 34:02.680
She's only semi-retired.

34:02.760 --> 34:05.560
I bet the gentlemen
would find that funny, eh?

34:10.360 --> 34:13.720
Told to you in the spirit
of friendship and co-operation.

34:15.800 --> 34:17.480
See you Sunday.

34:18.600 --> 34:20.720
- Name?
- The Digbeth Kid.

34:21.000 --> 34:22.480
Your real name?

34:23.280 --> 34:24.840
Harold Hancox.

34:27.080 --> 34:28.240
Profession?

34:29.400 --> 34:31.000
Illegal bookmaker.

34:31.360 --> 34:33.040
You don't admit that.

34:33.280 --> 34:35.640
You say something
like 'umbrella mender'.

34:35.720 --> 34:39.560
And we decide you're lying
and then we arrest you.

34:40.040 --> 34:41.360
Umbrella mender.

34:44.160 --> 34:47.000
You were found in possession
of betting slips,

34:47.280 --> 34:49.760
race cards and ticker tape.

34:50.040 --> 34:51.600
Any explanation?

34:54.320 --> 34:55.520
No.

34:55.800 --> 34:59.760
"No, I have no explanation

35:00.040 --> 35:04.440
for my possession of
those items, Officer."

35:04.960 --> 35:08.080
Take him down will you?
I'll fill in the rest myself.

35:08.160 --> 35:09.440
Come on, son.

35:16.960 --> 35:18.760
Bloody hell fire.

35:35.000 --> 35:36.440
You've killed him, huh?

35:37.960 --> 35:40.440
As I've explained,
he's not easy to get at.

35:40.520 --> 35:42.320
He's got an army round him.

35:43.560 --> 35:45.680
But when the time is right...

35:47.000 --> 35:49.000
The time is right.
It is right now.

35:49.080 --> 35:52.560
When he's away from his
familiar territory, I'll take him.

35:54.920 --> 35:56.160
So what do you want?

35:56.240 --> 36:00.280
We heard from a guard that one of their
bookies has been put inside.

36:00.360 --> 36:01.920
Kid called Harold Hancox.

36:02.240 --> 36:03.840
We could make a point.

36:04.080 --> 36:07.800
How many bakers is there
in Camden Town now?

36:07.920 --> 36:11.000
There are a lot of bakers
in Camden Town, Mr. Sabini.

36:11.080 --> 36:14.800
But, as I have said, we don't
as yet have a definite number.

36:14.880 --> 36:18.080
- Who won the 4.15 At Chepstow?
- Ragman.

36:18.160 --> 36:20.680
- Where's my dry cleaning?
- It's collected.

36:20.760 --> 36:22.720
Who won the 3.50
at Kempton Park?

36:26.560 --> 36:29.000
- What? You don't know?
- I can find out.

36:29.080 --> 36:31.080
No need. I know, already.

36:35.240 --> 36:39.920
You see, the favorites...
They're all winning.

36:40.000 --> 36:43.160
And the second favorites
are being done.

36:44.320 --> 36:46.440
- He's bewitching them.
- Bewitching?

36:46.720 --> 36:49.640
Gypsy stuff. Herbs, potions.

36:51.080 --> 36:53.640
And he's tipping off our big punters.

36:53.920 --> 36:55.720
They know who's going to win.

36:56.640 --> 36:59.240
Regular customers
betraying us for profit.

36:59.320 --> 37:02.640
You'd think they'd be loyal to us.
But profit, you see...

37:07.120 --> 37:11.320
- Comes before race, creed and family.
- Results look normal to me.

37:11.400 --> 37:13.880
You see shit!

37:14.080 --> 37:18.280
Mr. Sabini, if you think they're
planning to fix Northern races,

37:18.360 --> 37:21.560
we'll take no more bets
on anything north of Towcester.

37:21.640 --> 37:25.000
- I gave you a job already.
- And the job will be done.

37:25.080 --> 37:27.360
- But meantime...
- 'Meantime' fuck.

37:29.080 --> 37:31.360
What do you want
us to do about the kid

37:31.440 --> 37:33.240
in the cage in Birmingham?

37:33.400 --> 37:34.680
[Sniffing]

37:41.520 --> 37:42.520
[gasps]

37:49.280 --> 37:52.080
Why would you want me
to say that out loud?

37:59.760 --> 38:01.680
Is there somebody listening?

38:08.080 --> 38:09.520
All right, I'll say it.

38:09.600 --> 38:12.400
We'll kill the Hancox kid
to send a message.

38:19.520 --> 38:20.680
Good.

38:21.280 --> 38:23.840
And then you deal with
Tommy fucking Shelby.

38:35.320 --> 38:36.640
[Knocking at door]

38:38.600 --> 38:40.720
You wanted to see me?

38:40.840 --> 38:41.960
Aye.

38:48.240 --> 38:49.720
Take off your clothes.

38:56.640 --> 38:58.280
I know what you are.

38:59.640 --> 39:03.080
So take off your clothes.

39:08.240 --> 39:12.120
Not done in a very gentlemanly
like way, if I may say.

39:13.480 --> 39:15.120
Indeed not.

39:23.520 --> 39:25.800
So it is true. Stop.

39:27.760 --> 39:30.080
I only wanted proof

39:30.160 --> 39:32.280
not satisfaction.

39:32.360 --> 39:34.520
Or indeed infection.

39:38.240 --> 39:40.120
Are you in on the joke?

39:41.240 --> 39:42.600
What joke?

39:45.560 --> 39:49.120
I have unfinished business
in this filthy city.

39:49.200 --> 39:53.400
And when the time comes,
I will finish that business.

39:53.480 --> 39:57.440
And then my wrath will
come burn on all of yous.

39:59.120 --> 40:00.400
Now,

40:01.800 --> 40:04.720
You tell your friends
in the police station,

40:05.760 --> 40:08.000
that I know.

40:09.920 --> 40:12.320
And when my judgment comes...

40:13.320 --> 40:15.240
I'll be the one that's laughing.

40:17.520 --> 40:18.800
Get out.

40:29.200 --> 40:30.520
[woman singing]

40:45.080 --> 40:46.600
[continues singing]

41:01.280 --> 41:02.800
[upbeat music playing]

41:13.280 --> 41:15.840
Put them in there on the right.
On the right.

41:16.640 --> 41:18.960
You ain't got all night.
Come on!

41:20.080 --> 41:22.720
Put them in there
on the right.

41:22.800 --> 41:26.080
On the right.
Come on, boys.

41:33.800 --> 41:34.920
Harold Hancox?

41:35.960 --> 41:38.520
[grunting]

41:41.200 --> 41:44.440
Tell everybody
this is what happens

41:44.520 --> 41:47.840
to 'Blinders' who come into
the Green from now on.

41:47.920 --> 41:49.760
Orders of Sabini.

41:49.840 --> 41:52.360
I'm not a Blinder.
I'm not a Blinder.

41:52.440 --> 41:54.280
I just stood up. I'm not real!

41:54.360 --> 41:57.120
I'm not real! I'm not real!

41:57.200 --> 41:58.680
[blood gurgling]

42:15.160 --> 42:17.920
This had better be good
to interrupt my holiday.

42:18.000 --> 42:20.240
- Where's the boy?
- In the back room.

42:20.320 --> 42:22.600
Afterwards we're going to the museum.

42:22.680 --> 42:25.440
- He wanted to come say hello-
- Shut up, John.

42:25.520 --> 42:28.160
There is nothing of interest
to Michael here.

42:30.560 --> 42:32.000
Tommy, get on with this.

42:35.800 --> 42:40.200
Last night, one of our men
had his throat cut in Winson Green.

42:40.280 --> 42:43.200
I had a telegram
saying it was Sabini's order.

42:43.280 --> 42:46.040
And it says here that
Thomas Shelby is next.

42:46.360 --> 42:48.800
If our men think we
can't look after them,

42:48.880 --> 42:50.320
they'll not work for us.

42:50.400 --> 42:51.800
Sabini knows that.

42:51.880 --> 42:54.160
We need to get
the Green sorted out.

42:54.240 --> 42:56.720
Scudboat, you and one of the boys.

42:56.800 --> 42:59.160
Brake some windows and get arrested.

42:59.240 --> 43:03.240
And if our coppers get you into the Green,
you find out who did it.

43:03.320 --> 43:05.360
Instead, can't we pinch a car?

43:05.440 --> 43:06.440
[all laughing]

43:06.520 --> 43:07.520
What?

43:07.600 --> 43:10.880
Everybody else is getting a car.
I'm still on a donkey.

43:11.120 --> 43:13.200
Just get yourselves arrested.

43:13.360 --> 43:16.160
It doesn't matter now.
And before you all laugh,

43:16.320 --> 43:18.360
a boy is dead.

43:18.480 --> 43:20.120
He was just a kid.

43:20.360 --> 43:22.680
We'll start a fund for his family, Pol.

43:22.760 --> 43:24.080
Agreed.

43:25.040 --> 43:26.720
So is that it? Can I go now?

43:26.800 --> 43:28.760
Well as company treasurer,

43:28.840 --> 43:32.560
I need your permission
to spend a thousand guineas.

43:32.640 --> 43:35.480
- On what?
- On a horse.

43:37.800 --> 43:39.840
A thousand guineas on a horse?

43:40.320 --> 43:41.760
That's right.

43:46.160 --> 43:48.520
When was this decided?

43:48.600 --> 43:50.680
You've been busy with Michael.

43:50.760 --> 43:52.200
Oh, my God!

43:52.880 --> 43:56.440
So in the absence of common sense,
you boys have had an idea.

43:56.520 --> 43:59.320
Polly, there's a thoroughbred
quarter Arab filly

43:59.400 --> 44:01.920
up for auction at
the Doncaster Blood Stock.

44:02.000 --> 44:04.680
What do we want with
a thousand guinea horse?

44:04.760 --> 44:07.640
When we make our move
on Sabini's racing pitches,

44:07.720 --> 44:12.000
any men we get in the betting enclosure
will be lifted by Sabini's police.

44:12.120 --> 44:15.320
A good racehorse is a passport
to the owners enclosure.

44:15.400 --> 44:17.640
We'll be in there
with all the toffs.

44:18.040 --> 44:20.280
The coppers won't know
where to look.

44:20.360 --> 44:22.040
The Epsom Derby Pol.

44:22.120 --> 44:24.040
We'll be drinking with the King.

44:24.120 --> 44:25.240
The Derby?

44:26.120 --> 44:27.680
Did he say the Derby?

44:32.240 --> 44:33.400
That's right.

44:33.480 --> 44:36.560
For the last 10 years,
Sabini's made it his race.

44:36.640 --> 44:39.560
If we're going to take him down,
make it a symbol.

44:39.640 --> 44:41.840
Did you come up with this
in the pub?

44:41.960 --> 44:45.800
Pol, a good racehorse
is an investment,

44:46.160 --> 44:47.600
like property.

44:47.960 --> 44:50.840
We need to diversify
the portfolio...

44:51.240 --> 44:53.280
- So when is this sale?
- Tomorrow.

44:53.360 --> 44:54.920
Tommy's had a death threat

44:55.000 --> 44:56.800
so we'll have to go with him.

44:57.040 --> 45:00.480
So, you're going to close up
the shop, go out on a piss up,

45:00.560 --> 45:03.840
blow 1000 guineas on a horse
that's not even whole Arab.

45:03.920 --> 45:06.840
[laughing] Quarter Arab
is better.

45:06.920 --> 45:10.320
- Quarter Arab it means...
- Curly, shut up.

45:13.520 --> 45:16.600
I thought I told you
to lock that door.

45:16.680 --> 45:19.120
He did. I used the key on the nail.

45:19.200 --> 45:21.600
Look. I've been listening.

45:21.680 --> 45:23.800
- I want to go with them.
- You see?

45:25.280 --> 45:28.240
I love horses.
I could even help.

45:28.560 --> 45:30.480
Over my dead body.

45:30.560 --> 45:32.440
It'll be all right, mum.

45:32.520 --> 45:35.480
I've been to loads of horse auctions
with my uncle.

45:35.560 --> 45:37.760
They're very respectable.

45:37.840 --> 45:39.560
People bring their butlers.

45:39.640 --> 45:41.840
Yeah and their posh wives...

45:41.920 --> 45:44.520
And their mistresses.

45:44.600 --> 45:46.080
Let him come, Polly.

45:47.480 --> 45:50.480
We'll go there, buy an horse,
come back.

45:50.560 --> 45:53.880
I'll drop him at the house
in Sutton before it gets dark.

45:55.200 --> 45:56.320
No.

45:58.720 --> 46:00.320
Fucking no.

46:12.680 --> 46:13.960
[door slams]

46:20.160 --> 46:21.880
All right, that's it.

46:21.960 --> 46:23.840
Back to work. Come on.

46:29.120 --> 46:32.440
Aunt Pol,
when I was Michael's age,

46:32.520 --> 46:35.680
I'd killed a hundred men
and seen a thousand die.

46:35.760 --> 46:38.520
If you want to scare away
that kid away forever,

46:38.600 --> 46:40.280
carry on how you're going.

46:40.360 --> 46:44.040
If you want him to stay,
let him come.

46:56.760 --> 46:58.280
I can't believe it.

46:58.360 --> 47:00.240
All John's old things fit me.

47:01.840 --> 47:03.840
Christ, you look like your father.

47:15.840 --> 47:17.960
Shrimp, ham.

47:24.920 --> 47:27.200
What was he like? My dad?

47:28.160 --> 47:29.480
How did he die?

47:32.400 --> 47:36.000
Well, I won't lie to you.

47:36.680 --> 47:40.040
He died drunk, squeezed
between a boat and a lock.

47:41.160 --> 47:43.200
A real river gypsy's death.

47:45.360 --> 47:46.640
But he could sing.

47:47.960 --> 47:49.160
Play the piano.

47:50.680 --> 47:52.760
His smile will break your heart.

47:54.800 --> 47:56.840
You've got his same beautiful eyes.

48:01.800 --> 48:04.360
When he was sober,
he was kind and gentle.

48:11.560 --> 48:15.440
His trouble was, he fell in with the wrong
crowd when he was a boy.

48:16.200 --> 48:17.920
[car horn blowing]

48:19.080 --> 48:23.640
I have told them not
to blow that horn.

48:23.960 --> 48:26.520
This is a respectable
fucking neighborhood.

48:48.160 --> 48:50.920
- She's heating up, Curly. Take a look.
- Yeah.

48:53.200 --> 48:54.760
Let me out for a piss.

48:57.000 --> 48:58.880
She made loads. Do you want one?

48:58.960 --> 49:01.560
- What the bloody hell's that?
- Sandwiches.

49:01.640 --> 49:04.080
Ham, I think.
We've got shrimp paste too.

49:04.160 --> 49:08.040
There's tea. We'll have to take turns
'cause there's only one cup.

49:09.240 --> 49:11.480
- What?
- Sandwiches?

49:12.760 --> 49:15.280
- Yeah.
- Polly made bloody sandwiches?

49:15.360 --> 49:17.320
What's this? Teddy bear's picnic?

49:17.400 --> 49:20.680
All right. We will drink the tea,
eat the sandwiches.

49:20.760 --> 49:24.040
Then we will drive on.
All right? No crumbs, Charlie.

49:25.400 --> 49:27.400
- Come on.
- Herd them up, Arthur.

49:27.480 --> 49:29.240
- You fat bastard.
- Fuck off.

49:29.320 --> 49:30.680
John! Come on!

49:31.520 --> 49:33.440
This is a respectable event

49:33.520 --> 49:35.520
and you'll behave accordingly.

49:35.640 --> 49:37.240
No weapons, no drinking.

49:37.320 --> 49:39.720
John, we will stay together.

49:39.800 --> 49:41.920
When our horse comes up,
I'll bid.

49:42.000 --> 49:44.200
I've registered with the auctioneer,

49:44.280 --> 49:45.920
he knows to expect my bids.

49:46.040 --> 49:48.000
Do I get to run a hand over her?

49:48.080 --> 49:51.360
We have a vet's report. Keep an eye open
when she walks.

49:51.440 --> 49:53.080
Something isn't right.

49:53.160 --> 49:56.040
You're just in an unfamiliar place.
We all are.

49:56.120 --> 49:57.640
I get feelings sometimes.

49:57.720 --> 49:59.080
It's all right, Curly.

49:59.160 --> 50:00.200
Shut up, Curly.

50:00.280 --> 50:01.880
[announcer] Do I hear 1450?

50:01.960 --> 50:05.040
Do I hear 500, 500, 1600.

50:05.120 --> 50:07.120
1700, thank you.

50:07.200 --> 50:09.480
1900, 2000,

50:09.560 --> 50:12.240
2100, 2200.

50:12.320 --> 50:14.680
Why do people come late
to these things?

50:14.760 --> 50:16.040
Mmm?

50:16.120 --> 50:18.120
'Cause they know what they want.

50:18.200 --> 50:20.160
Only you need to see every horse.

50:20.240 --> 50:22.160
[announcer] 2700, am I bid?

50:22.240 --> 50:24.960
2600, I am bid.

50:26.800 --> 50:27.840
Sold!

50:28.040 --> 50:30.400
Morals of Marcus,
Sedgemere stud,

50:30.520 --> 50:32.880
by Tetrach out of Lady Josephine.

50:32.960 --> 50:35.240
We'll start the bidding
at 800 guineas.

50:35.320 --> 50:37.080
All right, Tommy, this is her.

50:51.080 --> 50:53.560
[announcer] Do we hear 800
hundred guineas?

50:54.240 --> 50:56.440
Eight fifty?

50:56.520 --> 51:00.560
Eight fifty? Do I hear nine?

51:00.640 --> 51:03.720
- Do I hear nine? Nine, am I bid?
- Bid for this one.

51:03.800 --> 51:06.240
- The gray? Why?
- Nine fifty.

51:06.320 --> 51:08.320
- He's beautiful.
- It's a filly.

51:08.400 --> 51:10.600
Micky needs some pace
for the gallops.

51:10.680 --> 51:13.440
One thousand, do I have
one thousand and fifty?

51:14.200 --> 51:16.520
One thousand one hundred...

51:16.600 --> 51:19.240
One thousand two hundred.

51:19.320 --> 51:22.480
Do I hear two fifty?

51:22.560 --> 51:24.040
Two fifty?

51:24.120 --> 51:26.960
I'll go halves with you
from the Spring fund.

51:27.040 --> 51:29.640
One thousand three hundred.

51:29.720 --> 51:32.160
One thousand four hundred.

51:32.240 --> 51:34.200
Do I hear five?

51:34.280 --> 51:35.920
One thousand five hundred.

51:36.000 --> 51:39.760
One thousand six hundred.
Do I hear seven?

51:39.840 --> 51:41.640
One thousand seven hundred.

51:41.720 --> 51:42.880
You have to stop.

51:42.960 --> 51:44.840
You don't have it in your belt.

51:44.920 --> 51:46.680
I'm having the horse.

51:46.760 --> 51:50.200
She's a sweet beast, Tom.
But stop. I feel something bad.

51:50.280 --> 51:51.480
Shut up, Curly!

51:51.560 --> 51:55.000
Two thousand,
two thousand and fifty?

51:56.000 --> 51:58.520
Two thousand and fifty.
Am I bid?

51:58.600 --> 52:00.640
- Two thousand and fifty?
- Enough.

52:00.720 --> 52:03.760
- Billy can use my geldings.
- 2000, I 'm bid.

52:03.840 --> 52:07.920
Do I hear two thousand and fifty? 
Last time.

52:08.000 --> 52:10.640
Two thousand and fifty?

52:12.120 --> 52:14.280
Sold to Mister?

52:14.360 --> 52:16.640
Thomas Shelby.

52:18.200 --> 52:20.360
[chattering]

52:31.920 --> 52:34.600
To the premises of
Mr. Charles Strong.

52:34.920 --> 52:37.120
Small Heath.
What kind of premises?

52:37.320 --> 52:39.720
- It's a boat yard.
- Yachts?

52:40.560 --> 52:41.920
Canals.

52:44.520 --> 52:47.240
I'm curious. What is
your business, Mr. Shelby?

52:47.320 --> 52:48.920
Import, export.

52:51.520 --> 52:54.960
But I also sell pegs
and tell fortunes.

53:00.800 --> 53:03.520
You beat us to it.

53:03.600 --> 53:04.840
Did I?

53:04.920 --> 53:07.240
I was trying to nab
a filly for my stud.

53:08.760 --> 53:11.400
- I'm sorry.
- Thomas Shelby, from where?

53:13.400 --> 53:15.720
- From Birmingham.
- Goodness.

53:16.000 --> 53:18.120
No. Not much.

53:18.440 --> 53:19.440
May Carleton.

53:19.840 --> 53:22.920
I breed racehorses and train them.
What is it you do?

53:23.360 --> 53:25.600
I rarely answer questions
is what I do.

53:25.680 --> 53:29.960
Tommy! Come on, hurry up! We got to go!

53:30.080 --> 53:32.640
If you ever decided to put that filly out,

53:32.720 --> 53:33.920
I'd be interested.

53:34.000 --> 53:35.520
I'm planning to race her.

53:36.520 --> 53:38.960
- Do you have a trainer?
- I know people.

53:39.040 --> 53:40.720
- I know people too.
- Tommy!

53:40.800 --> 53:42.600
We've got to get this kid back

53:42.680 --> 53:46.000
- or Polly will have your balls!
- She will have 'em!

53:46.080 --> 53:48.480
We know different people,
I would guess.

53:48.560 --> 53:50.760
My father knows Mick Hancock.

53:50.840 --> 53:54.320
- He trained three Ascot winners.
- That was your father?

53:56.600 --> 53:59.920
Yes. We're joint owners of the stud.

54:00.280 --> 54:03.440
He took the majority share
when my husband was killed.

54:03.520 --> 54:05.520
- Ypres.
- Tommy!

54:05.720 --> 54:08.080
We got to get back
to the caravans!

54:08.520 --> 54:10.760
The chickens are hungry.

54:10.840 --> 54:12.840
[chuckles]

54:14.480 --> 54:15.920
So will you consider me?

54:18.200 --> 54:19.440
I will consider you.

54:21.680 --> 54:23.800
You still didn't tell me
what you do.

54:24.680 --> 54:26.000
Oh, I do bad things.

54:27.640 --> 54:29.200
But you already know that.

54:31.880 --> 54:34.600
Right, about bloody time!

54:35.560 --> 54:38.440
[giggling and laughing]

54:38.960 --> 54:42.040
It's the fucking truth, John.
Rich women these days,

54:42.120 --> 54:44.960
all they want is working class cock.

54:45.040 --> 54:48.400
Maybe she was the something bad
I had the feeling about...

54:48.480 --> 54:50.520
She looks all right to me, Curly.

54:50.600 --> 54:53.600
All their men are dead, see?
The officers all shot.

54:53.680 --> 54:54.840
Yeah, by us.

54:54.920 --> 54:57.520
She has good contacts in the racing world.

54:57.600 --> 54:59.960
- Michael, you drive.
- Thomas Shelby?

55:00.040 --> 55:01.080
Tommy!

55:01.160 --> 55:02.680
[gunshots]

55:03.120 --> 55:06.360
Tommy! Down! Down!

55:07.920 --> 55:10.480
Tommy Shelby?
How about fucking Arthur?

55:10.560 --> 55:13.200
Fucking good to meet you! Arthur!

55:13.280 --> 55:15.720
Arthur! Arthur! Arthur!

55:17.320 --> 55:20.040
- Get him off! Arthur!
- Stop it, Arthur! Stop!

55:20.120 --> 55:23.800
Get him off! Get him off!
Arthur, that's it.

55:23.880 --> 55:25.600
[screaming]

55:25.680 --> 55:28.920
- Get him off! Get him off!
- Get him off!

55:29.960 --> 55:32.600
Come on! Come on!

55:32.680 --> 55:36.040
That's enough. Stop it!

55:36.120 --> 55:39.560
That's enough. Stop!

55:39.640 --> 55:42.960
- Shut up!
- Okay.

55:43.040 --> 55:46.000
Come on, come on. Get him up.

55:48.800 --> 55:52.120
- He's still breathing.
- Don't get blood on the kid.

55:52.200 --> 55:55.240
Michael, you didn't see a thing.
This didn't happen.

55:55.320 --> 55:57.400
All right? Give me the keys.

55:57.480 --> 55:59.000
Michael, give me the keys.

55:59.080 --> 56:01.000
I'm all right to drive.

56:03.000 --> 56:04.440
All right. Go on. Go on.

56:04.520 --> 56:06.320
Tommy, he's fucking scarpered!

56:06.400 --> 56:08.840
Right, let's get out of here, sharpish.

56:08.920 --> 56:09.920
Come on!

56:15.640 --> 56:16.640
[door opens]

56:19.440 --> 56:20.360
[door closes]

56:24.360 --> 56:25.520
So?

56:26.960 --> 56:28.560
How did it go?

56:29.000 --> 56:33.160
It was cold in the van,
but, super. Really.

56:33.280 --> 56:34.760
Let me smell your breath.

56:34.840 --> 56:35.840
[blows]

56:38.560 --> 56:40.160
Did they behave themselves?

56:42.400 --> 56:44.120
They were amazing, mum.

56:45.120 --> 56:47.280
They were really amazing.

56:53.360 --> 56:55.320
[peppy music playing]

57:34.240 --> 57:35.520
[sighs]

58:03.720 --> 58:06.280
[theme song playing]
